    "created_at": "2026-11-20T21:53:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-20T21:53:43Z",
    "body": "> ```\r\n> <a href=\"example.com\" target=\"_self\" windowfeatures=\"popup\">example.com</a>\r\n> <a href=\"example.com\" target=\"_self\" windowfeatures=\"split\">example.com</a>\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> In each of the cases of the above, is it correct to understand that the \"target\" attribute is ignored (or overwritten with \"_blank\"), and that the linked content is displayed in popup window or split view?\r\n\r\nActually no, in both cases the `target=\"_self\"` takes precedence, and the current navigable is navigated. No popup/split will happen here. In this PR, \"get an element's target\" only substitutes `_blank` when the element has no target attribute. With target=\"_self\", the existing step above it sets target to _self, so the new branch never runs. This all matches `window.open()` today, e.g. `window.open(url, \"_self\", \"popup\")` doesn't generate a new popup.",
